# NOTICE

dsh-see-act is MIT licensed. It stands on two other projects:

## dsh-plugin-real-input (MIT)

`lib/win32ps.mjs` started from the PowerShell + P/Invoke approach used by
[dsh-plugin-real-input](https://github.com/) and keeps its C# `DshNative`
declarations (SetCursorPos, mouse_event, SendInput, ShowWindow, EnumWindows,
GetWindowRect, AttachThreadInput, …) essentially unchanged, with three additions:

* `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext(-4)` is now called before *any* coordinate is
  read or written, and monitor geometry comes from `System.Windows.Forms.Screen`,
  so the plugin sees physical pixels on a scaled display;
* `FindWindowByPid` finds the see-now floating window even though tkinter
  windows created with `overrideredirect` have no title;
* the screen capture, the annotation and the input all run inside a single
  `powershell.exe` process per tool call.

If you have dsh-plugin-real-input installed, keep it: the two plugins do not
share state, and `real_*` remains the right tool for CDP-page clicks and
full-resolution screenshots.

## dsh-see-now (PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0)

see-act does not copy or modify see-now. It optionally imports
`dsh-see-now/chat` to write narration into the same floating window and reads
`~/.dsh/see-now-window.pid` to know where that window is, so a click that would
land behind it can step around it. Both are optional and degrade to no-ops when
see-now is not installed.

## FFmpeg

The optional black-screen fallback runs `ffmpeg` (from `DSH_FFMPEG_PATH` or the
`PATH`) once per affected call, with the same `ddagrab` filter see-now uses.
FFmpeg is not bundled.
